    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-23896",
      "knowledge_point_id": "240a0a0b-fb86-5760-bb2b-74275708ff77",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify ${sec}^{2}⁡x({cot}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses using the distributive property. This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x({cot}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x-{sec}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}-1 \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, using the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡x({cot}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x) & ={cot}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
